+# Hegel property-based testing report: `bitcode`
+
+Crate: `bitcode` 0.6.9 (SoftbearStudios/bitcode) — a compact, bit-packed binary
+serializer. Property tests were added with the `hegeltest` library (imported as
+`hegel`). All work is local; nothing was pushed.
+
+## 1. Setup and scope
+
+- Added `hegeltest` as a dev-dependency: `cargo add --dev hegeltest -p bitcode`.
+- Tests live in the existing public-API test file, `src/derive/mod.rs`, in a new
+  `#[cfg(all(test, feature = "derive", feature = "std"))] mod hegel_tests`
+  module alongside the crate's own `encode`/`decode` roundtrip tests. No new
+  test file was created (per hegel guidance).
+- The crate's test harness requires nightly (`#![cfg_attr(test, feature(test))]`
+  for benches), so run with:
+  `cargo +nightly test --lib hegel_tests`
+- `.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore`.
+- Maintainer AI-consent check: no governance/opt-out file
+  (`HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`, `.ai-ack`, anti-AI `CONTRIBUTING`) exists; the README
+  carries only the standard Apache/MIT contribution clause. Proceeded with local
+  testing only.
+- The crate ships a `cargo fuzz` target (`fuzz/fuzz_targets/fuzz.rs`) that
+  roundtrips a large type matrix through both the derive and serde paths. The
+  hegel suite supplements it by (a) generating a richly-nested value model with
+  boundary-biased primitives and shrinkable counterexamples, and (b) directly
+  targeting the attacker-controlled-length path with crafted streams rather than
+  hoping random fuzz bytes hit that structure.
+
+## 2. Properties added (10)
+
+All tests are `#[hegel::test]` and pass at the default case count. Roundtrip and
+crafted-length findings are oracle-independent (the test holds the value / the
+expected error), which is the highest-confidence class.
+
+1. **`rich_roundtrip`** (the crown): `decode(&encode(v)) == v` over a
+   deeply-nested `Rich` model — an enum spanning every integer width
+   (`u8..u128`, `i8..i128`, `usize`/`isize`, all covering MIN/MAX/0), `f32`/`f64`
+   (compared bit-exactly), `bool`, `char` (ASCII/BMP/supplementary), `String`
+   (full Unicode), `Option`, bounded `Vec`, tuples, arrays, `Box`, `BTreeMap`
+   with both integer and `String` keys, and structs nested in enums / enums
+   nested in structs.
+2. **`rich_determinism`**: encoding the same value twice, and via the reusable
+   `Buffer::encode` API, yields byte-identical output.
+3. **`arbitrary_bytes_no_panic`** (key untrusted target): `decode::<T>` of
+   arbitrary bounded bytes across 13 target types returns `Err` or a value —
+   never panics/OOMs/hangs; a recovered value must survive a re-roundtrip
+   (purity, no invalid internal state).
+4. **`corrupted_encoding_no_panic`**: a valid encoding with a flipped bit,
+   truncation, or appended bytes must decode to `Err` or a stable valid value.
+5. **`cross_type_decode_no_panic`**: bytes valid for one type, decoded as 8
+   unrelated types, never panic; any success must be stable.
+6. **`crafted_huge_length_rejected`** (key untrusted target): a stream whose
+   collection length field is crafted huge (`[255, 0, <len u64 LE>]`,
+   `len >= 1 MiB` up to `u64::MAX`) must be rejected without OOM, for
+   `Vec<u8/u16/u64/u128>`, `String`, and `Vec<Rich>`.
+7. **`integer_roundtrip_all_widths`**: per-width integer roundtrip, boundaries
+   included.
+8. **`float_roundtrip_bit_exact`**: `f32`/`f64` roundtrip is bit-exact including
+   subnormals, ±0, ±inf, and NaN payloads.
+9. **`string_roundtrip_unicode`**: `String` roundtrip over full Unicode text.
+10. **`collection_roundtrip`**: `Vec<i32>` at drawn sizes up to 300 (exercising
+    the large-length packing path) and `BTreeMap<u32,u64>` with unique keys.
+
+## 3. Findings
+
+No bugs found. The suite is green at the default count and stayed green in a
+2000-case (~20x) exploratory run (case count confirmed via an in-test counter).
+There are no failing/pinned tests.
+
+Positive robustness observations (oracle-independent):
+
+- **Attacker-controlled lengths are safely bounded.** A crafted huge length is
+  rejected before any large allocation: values at/above the crate's
+  `HUGE_LEN` cap (`0x7FFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF / 4096`, ~2^51) fail in
+  `LengthDecoder::populate` with `"huge length"`; values below the cap but
+  exceeding available input fail with `"EOF"` because element decode checks
+  `input.len()` before allocating (`consume_bytes`/`consume_byte_arrays`).
+  Allocation is thus tied to actual input size, not the declared length.
+- **Float bits (including NaN payloads and signed zero) roundtrip exactly**, so
+  the suite compares floats via `to_bits()` (mirroring the crate's own
+  `BitsEqualF32` fuzz helper).
+
+## 4. Process-aborting risks (OOM / hang / stack overflow)
+
+None triggered. No `#[ignore]`d reproducers were needed.
+
+- The huge-length OOM vector (the obvious concern for a bit-packed format with
+  attacker-controlled length fields) is explicitly defended and verified clean
+  in `crafted_huge_length_rejected`.
+- Input sizes in the arbitrary-bytes / corrupted / crafted-length properties are
+  bounded (e.g. `binary().max_size(4096)`); this protects the *test's* resources
+  and keeps failures shrinkable, not the crate's contract (the length-cap
+  analysis above shows the crate is safe on unbounded declared lengths too).
+- The model is intentionally non-recursive (see limitations), so decode of the
+  generated types has bounded depth; no stack-overflow class applies to it.
+
+## 5. Limitations and notes
+
+- **bitcode's columnar derive cannot represent self-recursive types.** The
+  generated `Encoder`/`Decoder` embeds each child (co)der inline, so a
+  `Box<Self>`, `Vec<Self>`, or `Option<Box<Self>>` field inside a derived type
+  produces an infinitely-sized (co)der and fails to compile with an `E0391`
+  layout cycle. This is a genuine format constraint (no fuzz target uses a
+  recursive type either), not a hegel issue. The rich model is therefore
+  deeply *nested* across three concrete types (`Leaf`, `Inner`, `Rich`) rather
+  than recursive, which still covers structs-in-enums, enums-in-structs, boxes,
+  vecs, and maps.
+- Tests require the nightly toolchain because the crate gates benches behind
+  `feature(test)`; this is pre-existing, not introduced here.
+- The serde integration (behind the non-default `serde` feature) was not
+  targeted; the derive API is the primary path and the existing fuzz target
+  already covers serde. This is a candidate area for future property tests.
+- Adding `hegeltest` inflates the test binary and pulls in a dev-dependency
+  tree; if upstreamed, the dev-dependency and its MSRV are the maintainer's call.
